    "math_academy_id": "q-76770",
    "knowledge_point_id": "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987450/assets/f1880dc62f774152ce5076a313928c0e8109ca174184704c5880891ed9243718.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{2}{5},$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above.",
    "worked_solution": "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=\\frac{2}{5}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{2x+4}{6x+8} \\\\ 2(6x+8) & =5(2x+4) \\\\ 12x+16 & =10x+20 \\\\ 12x-10x & =20-16 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x+4=2(2)+4=8, \\\\ AC & =6x+8=6(2)+8=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
